    "excerpt": "A decision checklist for education entrepreneurs who want more than a brand name and a launch kit.",
    "content": "A preschool franchise should be evaluated as both an education system and a business partnership. Before comparing logos, fees or promises, ask whether the model can help you deliver a consistent child experience, train your team and make sound operating decisions over several years.\n\n## 1. What exactly am I licensed to use?\n\nClarify brand rights, programme coverage, territory, duration, renewal terms and what happens if you later add daycare or additional programmes. Put the answer in the agreement—not only in a presentation.\n\n## 2. What does the curriculum look like in an ordinary week?\n\nAsk to see the rhythm of a real week: play, language, early numeracy, movement, art, stories, outdoor exploration and teacher observation. A curriculum is valuable only when a new teacher can understand and use it well.\n\n## 3. How are teachers prepared and supported?\n\nAsk about induction, ongoing professional development, observation, mentoring and replacement training. A launch workshop alone is not the same as a teacher-development system.\n\n## 4. What support continues after opening day?\n\nRequest a deliverables list: academic review, operational guidance, marketing resources, quality checks, parent-engagement support and escalation channels. Find out which support is scheduled and which is only available on request.\n\n## 5. What is the complete commercial structure?\n\nLook beyond the entry price. Ask about royalties, renewals, materials, technology, mandatory purchases, training, marketing contributions and taxes. Model the total cost across the full licence term.\n\n## 6. How much local flexibility is permitted?\n\nIndia is multilingual and locally diverse. Ask how the model adapts to local languages, festivals, parent expectations and community context while protecting core quality.\n\n## 7. What will I be responsible for?\n\nA responsible franchisor should be clear that the local operator still owns admissions, people management, compliance, fee collection, parent relationships and daily execution. Support should strengthen leadership, not create the illusion that the centre runs itself.\n\n> **A useful comparison method:** Compare three shortlisted brands on one page using the same headings: licence, curriculum, teacher development, ongoing support, local flexibility, complete cost and operator responsibilities.\n\n## Questions readers often ask\n\n### Should I choose the cheapest franchise?\n\nNot automatically. Compare the complete cost and the usable support you receive. A low entry fee can still become expensive if essential services are separate or weak.\n\n### Can an existing preschool use the same checklist?\n\nYes. Add questions about rebranding, continuity for current families, existing staff, retained assets and the transition timetable.\n\n## Compare KIPS with the same questions\n\nAsk KIPS for a current scope of support and use this checklist during your franchise or ReBrand discussion.\n\n**[Book a Franchise / ReBrand Conversation →](/#partnerships)**",
