    "id": "journal-preschool-safety-hygiene-parent-checklist",
    "title": "Preschool Safety: What Parents Should Actually Look for During a School Visit",
    "slug": "preschool-safety-hygiene-parent-checklist",
    "category": "Early Pedagogy & Parent Trust",
    "tags": [
      "Early Pedagogy & Parent Trust",
      "Parenting",
      "Early Childhood"
    ],
    "status": "Published",
    "excerpt": "A practical tour checklist covering supervision, entry and exit, hygiene, child-friendly spaces and the everyday routines behind a safe preschool.",
    "content": "Safety is not created by a CCTV sticker at the entrance. Parents should look for the routines, environment and adult behaviour that reduce risk throughout the day—from arrival and toileting to classroom movement, outdoor play and authorised pickup.\n\n## Start at the gate\n\nObserve who can enter, how visitors are handled and how children are handed over at arrival and departure. Ask how authorised pickups are recorded and what happens if a different adult arrives unexpectedly.\n\nA secure entrance should still feel welcoming; safety and warmth are not opposites.\n\n## Look at the classroom from a child’s height\n\nCheck for sharp edges, unstable furniture, exposed wiring, easy access to hazardous cleaning products and cluttered walkways. Materials should be age-appropriate and stored so that children can use suitable resources safely.\n\nAsk how teachers supervise transitions, because many minor accidents happen while groups are moving rather than during the main activity.\n\n## Hygiene is a routine, not a deep-clean event\n\nNotice handwashing access, toilet cleanliness, drinking-water arrangements, cleaning schedules, food handling and what happens when a child becomes unwell during the day.\n\nThe school should be able to explain its routine without needing to prepare a special answer for the tour.\n\n## Ask how incidents are documented and communicated\n\nEven well-run schools can have bumps, bites or unexpected situations. What matters is whether staff respond promptly, give appropriate first aid, document what happened, inform families and review preventable causes.\n\nParents can ask who is trained for emergencies and how staff practise fire and evacuation procedures.\n\n## Use CCTV as one layer, not the whole safety system\n\nCCTV may support monitoring and review, subject to the school’s privacy and access policy. It cannot replace adequate staffing, active supervision, secure handovers, staff screening, safe facilities or a culture in which concerns are reported quickly.\n\n> **Five-minute safety scan:** Gate • toilets • classroom floor • outdoor area • pickup process. At each point ask: Who is supervising? What could a child reach? What happens if something goes wrong?\n\n## Questions readers often ask\n\n### Should parents expect unrestricted live CCTV access?\n\nPolicies vary. Ask the school to explain how CCTV is used, who can access recordings, how privacy is protected and how footage is handled when an incident needs review.\n\n### Is a very colourful classroom necessarily child-friendly?\n\nNo. Safety, usable space, calm organisation, accessible materials and attentive adults matter more than decoration.\n\n## Tour the routines, not only the rooms\n\nDuring a Kinderbee visit, ask the centre team to explain arrival, hygiene, supervision, incident response and authorised pickup procedures.\n\n**[Plan a Kinderbee Centre Visit →](/#contact)**",
